{
 "id": "WM-04",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-wm-04-138a938802",
 "endReason": "concluded",
 "customerTurns": 5,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Samsung WW90T534DAW keeps coming up with 4C after a couple of minutes. Drum is dry. Any ideas before I call someone out?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "4C on your Samsung means: Water supply issue - little or no water entering (tap/pressure/inlet mesh/hose). From what you have described, the most likely cause is power-cycle / reset. Next: Power-cycle the machine to clear a transient glitch. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, I've switched it off and on at the wall but I'm not sure that's the issue. It still won't take any water. What else can I check?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Check that the water taps are fully open and the inlet hoses are not kinked. Ensure the machine is connected to a cold water supply, as this error often indicates no water is entering the drum. From what you have described, the most likely cause is blocked inlet mesh / filter. Check the tap the fill hose connects to is turned fully on, and that the hose behind the machine isn't kinked or squashed against the wall. Was the tap fully on and the hose clear, did you find it off or kinked (and sort it), or is the hose itself damaged?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Where the fill-hose mesh filter is\" — A small mesh filter sits inside the fill-hose connector at the tap/valve end and catches grit. Turn the tap off, unscrew the hose and rinse the mesh. Typical layout — your machine may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Tap was open, I've turned it fully anticlockwise anyway and no change. Hose isn't kinked. Kitchen cold tap pressure is normal. Should I take the hose off and check the mesh filter?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you should disconnect the water inlet hose to clean the small mesh filters inside the machine’s water connections. This is a standard step for resolving error code 4C when water supply issues are suspected. Where the fill hose screws onto the back of the machine there's a small mesh filter that can block with grit or scale. Unscrew the hose at the machine end and look into the inlet. Was the mesh blocked (and is it clean now), or was it already clean?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, tap off and hose unscrewed. The mesh filter is clogged with grey grit and bits of limescale. Cleaning it now - anything else I should do before I put it back?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "You have already completed the necessary checks by cleaning the clogged inlet filters. There are no further safe actions you can take without professional assistance, so you may now reconnect and test the machine. From what you have described, the most likely cause is a blocked inlet mesh filter. Now turn the tap back on and start a programme. Does it fill normally now, or is it still not taking water?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Cleaned it and reconnected, tap back on. Started a wash and it's filling normally now, 4C has gone. The water company was working in the street two days ago and the water ran brown briefly, so I guess that's where the grit came from."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Good news, that has sorted it. If it happens again, start with the same check. Is there anything else I can help with?"
  }
 ],
